chu valley lake is one of the largest  reservoirs in the country  supplying water to nearly a million  people in bristol and somerset  it's a haven for wildlife but also a  critical barometer  of a natural resource which because of  climate change  even the uk can't take for granted our  winters are getting wetter  and our summer's drier it has a  significant effect on our water supply  and it's the uncertainty that is the  problem trying to manage around that  uncertainty  not knowing what's going to actually  happen in the future  is very challenging for the water  industry as a whole  whilst parts of the planet are already  facing dire water shortages  unless we conserve our supplies it's a  matter of time  before restrictions could be in place in  britain too  the reservoir is currently at a healthy  level but in the worst case drought  scenario  without action there could be water  shortages within  15 years the drought of 1976 brought  standpipes in the streets  and calls for people to share baths more  than 40 years on and  regular summer heat waves prompt a surge  in demand for water to drink and  use we filmed these pictures of somerset  prone to flooding a few weeks ago but  storing and moving water is complex  the chair of the environment agency says  they're planning for an overhaul of  water management  if we don't all work together  on the issue of water resources by  2050 we could find parts of this country  living under severe water stress does  severe water stress mean  maybe even stand pipes like we saw in  the 70s that could  be through other forms of water supply  than being able to turn on your tap such  as  bottled water potentially  communal taps  the environment agency says water  leakage needs to be dramatically reduced  and it isn't ruling out the possibility  that water bills could rise  what's critical is that consumers learn  to use  water wisely before the drain on our  natural assets becomes  too great lisa holland sky news somerset
 

